|~ What is declared under `::tagged`, the kind of every value under
   a tag, which the walk of a tagged value reaches after its own tags: the
   verbs of tagged values. ~|

:tagged/payload
  |~~ The value the tag holds, the reverse of `tag`: `value | tag ::Foo
      | payload | eq value` holds of every value. A set's payload is its
      vector.

      `payload` takes a value under a tag; a value of a kind of the
      core, a number or a plain map, stands under none.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:tagged/payload/answersAVector
  |~~ A box of a vector answers the vector. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[42] | payload | eq [42])

:tagged/payload/answersEveryElement
  |~~ A pair answers both its elements. ~~|
  ::law~(:Pair |~~ A pair. ~~| | ::Pair[1 2] | payload | eq [1 2])

:tagged/payload/answersAString
  |~~ A note of a string answers the string. ~~|
  ::law~(:Note |~~ A note. ~~| | ::Note"hello" | payload | eq "hello")

:tagged/payload/answersTheVectorOfASet
  |~~ A set's payload is its vector in its one order. ~~|
  ::law~(#[2 1] | payload | eq [1 2])

:tagged/payload/refusesAnUntaggedValue
  |~~ A number under no tag has no payload. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | payload !| [type /actualType] | eq [::tagged/payload/SubjectNotTaggedError ::number])

:tagged/within
  |~~ An edit under one tag: `tagged | within ~(…)` runs the quote
      against the payload as `apply` runs it, a fork whose declarations
      stay inside, and the answer returns the subject's kind, minted
      back under its tag, whose constructor runs once, at the rewrap.
      The steps between may break the tag's invariant,
      since an invariant holds of the result: a set's constructor
      normalizes the vector the edit answers, where a record's refuses
      it. An error the edit answers passes as it is, and a deeper stack
      of tags is reached by nesting `within`.

      `within` edits the value beneath a tag and puts the tag back; a
      value under no tag is edited by `apply`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:tagged/within/editsASetAsItsVector
  |~~ A set is edited as its vector and comes back a set. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1] | within ~([/0 /1 /0]) | eq #[1 3])

:tagged/within/keepsTheTag
  |~~ The edit returns under the tag. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2] | within ~(reverse) | eq ::Box[2 1])

:tagged/within/reachesUnderTwoTags
  |~~ An edit under two tags reaches the innermost payload. ~~|
  ::law~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box#[3 1] | within ~(within ~([/1 /0 2])) | eq ::Box#[1 2 3])

:tagged/within/refusesAnUntaggedValue
  |~~ A vector under no tag is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | within ~(reverse) !| [type /actualType] | eq [::tagged/within/SubjectNotTaggedError ::vec])

:tagged/within/code
  |~~ The code the call runs.

      The edit `within` runs is a quote, `within ~(reverse)`; a value
      computed at the call is refused, the error a step answered there
      among them.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:tagged/within/code/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(#[3 1] | within 1 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::tagged/within/code/NotQuoteError ::number])

:tagged/within/code/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The code is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:tagged/within/answer
  |~~ The value edited beneath its tag, under the tag again. ~~|
  ::tagged
